# Copilot Sonnet 4.6 Prompt — Common Audit Framework Across .NET Microservices

## Role

Act as a Principal Software Architect specializing in .NET 10, distributed systems, microservices, AWS, structured audit logging, and enterprise observability.

This workspace contains multiple independent solutions and `.csproj` projects. These applications perform different POST actions against records stored in DynamoDB. They are logically isolated, similar to independently deployable microservices.

I need you to design and implement a common, reusable audit framework that can be consumed by all of these applications.

The objective is:

> Every application must generate the same standardized audit information for record-level actions and write it to the same centralized audit/Splunk pipeline, with no application-specific variation in the audit contract.

The framework must be reusable, centrally governed, strongly typed, extensible, and production-ready.

---

## 1. Analyze Before Changing Anything

Before modifying code:

1. Scan the entire workspace.
2. Identify all `.sln`, `.slnx`, and `.csproj` files.
3. Identify each independent application/service.
4. Identify POST/action endpoints in each application.
5. Identify where DynamoDB records are read/updated.
6. Identify existing logging, audit, telemetry, correlation ID, request ID, or Splunk integration.
7. Identify any application that already has an audit implementation.
8. Identify differences between applications in how actions, statuses, errors, users, and record IDs are represented.
9. Produce a short architecture assessment before implementation.

Do not immediately start refactoring. First understand the existing architecture and dependencies.

