## 15. Idempotency

Step Functions can retry.

Example:

```text
Step Function
     |
POST API
     |
DynamoDB update succeeds
     |
Network response fails
     |
Step Function retries
```

The audit framework must distinguish:

- duplicate invocation
- legitimate retry
- new business action

Evaluate identifiers such as:

```text
operationId
recordId
action
attempt
eventId
workflowExecutionId
```

Implement an appropriate idempotency strategy.

---

## 16. Failure Handling

Analyze both cases:

```text
Business update succeeds
Audit write fails
```

and:

```text
Audit write succeeds
Business update fails
```

Clearly distinguish:

- action requested
- action started
- action succeeded
- action failed

Never report `SUCCESS` before the business operation actually succeeds.

If guaranteed audit delivery is required, evaluate an appropriate reliable mechanism such as an outbox/event-driven pattern. Do not introduce unnecessary complexity without evidence that it is required.

---

## 17. Security

Never log:

- passwords
- JWTs/access tokens
- secrets
- connection strings
- credentials
- complete file contents
- unnecessary sensitive/PII data

Do not log the complete DynamoDB record by default.

Log only fields required for auditability and troubleshooting.

---

## 18. Performance

The system may process millions of records.

Therefore:

- Avoid expensive synchronous external calls for every record.
- Prefer the existing asynchronous logging pipeline where available.
- Avoid loading entire batches into memory.
- Support asynchronous processing.
- Avoid excessive allocations.
- Evaluate batching where supported.
- Preserve one logical audit event per record even if transport batching is used.
- Ensure audit failures do not create uncontrolled retry loops.

